REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w12","title":"Pragmatic competence, context, writing at work and interviews","summary":"Grammar gives accuracy, but communication also needs the context: pragmatic competence, the barriers that distort a message, and Hymes's SPEAKING model. The rest of this last week is writing and speaking for work: essays and research reports, decent e-mails, a résumé, and job interviews (the STAR method and \"Tell me about yourself\").","n":12,"lectures":[{"code":"12.1","title":"Pragmatic competence vs communicative confidence","video":"ZoHS2K9jmzk","seconds":3321},{"code":"12.2","title":"Writing an essay or a research report","video":"rTdNZ0qYvwM","seconds":2220},{"code":"12.3","title":"Decent e-mails","video":"HwlmwClEmao","seconds":1961},{"code":"12.4","title":"Barriers to communication","video":"zCOp_u_2j6Q","seconds":2395},{"code":"12.5","title":"Context of Communication- The Speaking Model","video":"MK6jVE62F0M","seconds":2090},{"code":"12.6","title":"Writing resume","video":"shVQy2W_Izs","seconds":608},{"code":"12.7","title":"Position yourself to win - interview skills","video":"Izrp2Vdgphk","seconds":745},{"code":"12.8","title":"Interview skills - 1","video":"eykZsE5A02Q","seconds":2012},{"code":"12.9","title":"Interview skills- 2","video":"jacrUXc1bTU","seconds":2335},{"code":"12.10","title":"Tell me about yourself","video":"y4SvIzrUIOQ","seconds":500}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"pragmatic-competence-and-communicative-confidence","level":2,"title":"Pragmatic competence and communicative confidence"},{"id":"four-kinds-of-competence","level":3,"title":"Four kinds of competence"},{"id":"i-language-and-e-language","level":3,"title":"I-language and E-language"},{"id":"learning-a-language-is-learning-to-learn","level":3,"title":"Learning a language is learning to learn"},{"id":"speaking-is-instinctive-writing-is-learnt","level":3,"title":"Speaking is instinctive; writing is learnt"},{"id":"chomsky-from-habits-to-a-generative-mind","level":3,"title":"Chomsky: from habits to a generative mind"},{"id":"context-is-built-into-a-language","level":3,"title":"Context is built into a language"},{"id":"request-or-order-the-context-decides","level":3,"title":"Request or order? The context decides"},{"id":"saying-no-without-saying-no","level":3,"title":"Saying no without saying no"},{"id":"writing-an-essay-or-a-research-report","level":2,"title":"Writing an essay or a research report"},{"id":"listening-speaking-and-the-soft-skills","level":3,"title":"Listening, speaking and the soft skills"},{"id":"what-writing-is","level":3,"title":"What writing is"},{"id":"make-writing-a-habit","level":3,"title":"Make writing a habit"},{"id":"steps-to-write-well","level":3,"title":"Steps to write well"},{"id":"know-your-type-and-your-reader","level":3,"title":"Know your type and your reader"},{"id":"writing-a-research-report","level":3,"title":"Writing a research report"},{"id":"impressive-is-not-ornamental","level":3,"title":"Impressive is not ornamental"},{"id":"decent-e-mails","level":2,"title":"Decent e-mails"},{"id":"the-parts-of-an-e-mail","level":3,"title":"The parts of an e-mail"},{"id":"subject-lines-and-salutations","level":3,"title":"Subject lines and salutations"},{"id":"useful-phrases","level":3,"title":"Useful phrases"},{"id":"spacing","level":3,"title":"Spacing"},{"id":"rules-before-you-press-send","level":3,"title":"Rules before you press Send"},{"id":"barriers-to-communication","level":2,"title":"Barriers to communication"},{"id":"the-communication-cycle","level":3,"title":"The communication cycle"},{"id":"1-physical-and-mechanical-barriers","level":3,"title":"1. Physical and mechanical barriers"},{"id":"2-language-or-semantic-barriers","level":3,"title":"2. Language or semantic barriers"},{"id":"3-socio-psychological-barriers","level":3,"title":"3. Socio-psychological barriers"},{"id":"4-organizational-barriers","level":3,"title":"4. Organizational barriers"},{"id":"5-personal-barriers","level":3,"title":"5. Personal barriers"},{"id":"building-good-communication","level":3,"title":"Building good communication"},{"id":"context-of-communication-the-speaking-model","level":2,"title":"Context of communication: the SPEAKING model"},{"id":"from-linguistic-to-communicative-competence","level":3,"title":"From linguistic to communicative competence"},{"id":"formality-register-and-idiolect","level":3,"title":"Formality, register and idiolect"},{"id":"sixteen-components-and-the-speaking-acronym","level":3,"title":"Sixteen components and the SPEAKING acronym"},{"id":"writing-a-re-sume","level":2,"title":"Writing a résumé"},{"id":"the-overview","level":3,"title":"The overview"},{"id":"position-yourself-to-win-the-star-method","level":2,"title":"Position yourself to win: the STAR method"},{"id":"dos-and-don-ts","level":3,"title":"Dos and don’ts"},{"id":"interview-skills-before-during-and-after","level":2,"title":"Interview skills: before, during and after"},{"id":"the-challenges","level":3,"title":"The challenges"},{"id":"three-stages","level":3,"title":"Three stages"},{"id":"before-the-interview","level":3,"title":"Before the interview"},{"id":"during-the-interview","level":3,"title":"During the interview"},{"id":"after-the-interview","level":3,"title":"After the interview"},{"id":"linguistic-skills-interactive-skills-and-strategies","level":3,"title":"Linguistic skills, interactive skills and strategies"},{"id":"two-mock-interviews","level":2,"title":"Two mock interviews"},{"id":"demonstration-1-an-english-teacher","level":3,"title":"Demonstration 1: an English teacher"},{"id":"demonstration-2-a-project-manager","level":3,"title":"Demonstration 2: a project manager"},{"id":"what-the-board-was-looking-for","level":3,"title":"What the board was looking for"},{"id":"tell-me-about-yourself","level":2,"title":"“Tell me about yourself”"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pragmatic competence</strong> (12.1): learning a language means learning its grammar (<strong>linguistic competence</strong>, the I-language inside the mind) and its use in context (<strong>pragmatic competence</strong>, seen in performance, the E-language) at the same time. Together with communicative competence they build <strong>communicative confidence</strong>. Hindi examples show context built into a language: <em>raajuu <strong>ke</strong> do bacce haiN</em> but <em>raajuu <strong>ke paas</strong> do gaariyaaN haiN</em> (English uses <em>has</em> for both), and <em>maiN-ne kab kahaa?</em> (“When did I say?”) means “I did not say so”.</li>\n<li><strong>Essays and research reports</strong> (12.2): speaking is instinctive; writing is learnt with effort, and the reader has only your words. Give your writing a structure, outline it, answer the reader’s questions, drop the fillers, then edit and re-read. A research report goes from the problem and the method to the evidence and the conclusion; research itself is <strong>observation → description → explanation</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Decent e-mails</strong> (12.3): neat, short and crisp. Your name in the <em>From</em> line, a clear subject line, a salutation (<em>Dear Karen</em> or <em>Dear Dr Jones</em>, never <em>Dear Dr Karen</em>), a succinct body, a closing and a signature, with an empty line between the parts. Never reply in anger; read every e-mail three times.</li>\n<li><strong>Barriers to communication</strong> (12.4): “the greatest illusion about communication is that it has taken place”. Five kinds: <strong>physical and mechanical</strong>, <strong>language or semantic</strong>, <strong>socio-psychological</strong>, <strong>organizational</strong> and <strong>personal</strong>. The cure is a two-way, you-oriented, socio-culturally appropriate exchange.</li>\n<li><strong>The SPEAKING model</strong> (12.5): knowing a language means knowing its forms <strong>and</strong> its functions. Dell Hymes grouped 16 components of a conversation under eight letters: <strong>S</strong>etting and scene, <strong>P</strong>articipants, <strong>E</strong>nds, <strong>A</strong>ct sequence, <strong>K</strong>ey, <strong>I</strong>nstrumentalities, <strong>N</strong>orms, <strong>G</strong>enre. In short: who speaks what to whom, when, why and how.</li>\n<li><strong>Résumé</strong> (12.6): a CV is your full history; a <strong>résumé</strong> is its short version, one or two pages. Name and contact, a precise overview (no self-praise), publications, research experience (with varied verbs), awards, professional experience, education and skills, positions of responsibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Interviews</strong> (12.7–12.10): answer behavioural questions with <strong>STAR</strong> (Situation, Task, Action, Result) and show <strong>suitability</strong>, not just eligibility. Prepare before, perform during, follow up after. An interview has four parts: ice-breaking, technical, adaptability, winding up. <strong>“Tell me about yourself”</strong> = a one-sentence introduction + three to five sentences of achievements with proof + why you want this job.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"pragmatic-competence-and-communicative-confidence\">Pragmatic competence and communicative confidence</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.1 (Rajesh Kumar) shows the idea that has run under the whole course. These are things “teachers must know”, but the lecturer wants learners to know them too, “so that you can see, evaluate, understand and apply them to your own learning” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The starting point is <strong>accuracy</strong>. Accuracy comes from understanding structure, and “only with maintaining accuracy, we are going to sound confident” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). But grammar alone is not enough. We must also know how the language is used in its social and cultural <strong>context</strong> (the situation and the people in it). The lecture calls that knowledge <strong>pragmatic competence</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"four-kinds-of-competence\">Four kinds of competence</h3>\n<p><strong>Competence</strong> means knowledge or ability. Three of the four terms below are found in books on language; the fourth is the lecturer’s own (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Linguistic, pragmatic and communicative competence; communicative confidence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Linguistic competence</strong>, also called <strong>grammatical competence</strong>: knowing the structure of a language, the “subconscious computation” of sentences in the mind. (<em>Subconscious</em>: it happens without our noticing.) It gives <strong>grammatical accuracy</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Pragmatic competence</strong>: knowing what sentences mean when they are used in a real situation, with its social and cultural context. The lecture also calls it “discourse competence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3030s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 50:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Communicative competence</strong>: being able to use the language to communicate with others (Lecture 12.5 gives Dell Hymes’s full idea).</li>\n<li><strong>Communicative confidence</strong> (the lecturer’s own term): linguistic, communicative and pragmatic competence “together builds communicative confidence for the learners of a target language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). The <strong>target language</strong> is the new language you are learning, here English.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Week 2 met the first three in <a href=\"#/english2/w02/comment-viewpoint-and-focus-adverbs\">the lecture on adverbs</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"i-language-and-e-language\">I-language and E-language</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">I-language and E-language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I-language</strong> (internal language) is <strong>competence</strong>: the language inside the human mind, its subconscious processing, before we use it.</li>\n<li><strong>E-language</strong> (external language) is <strong>performance</strong>: the language we use to communicate. “The moment we start speaking, that becomes E-language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Objectives” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=335s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:35</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Language acquisition (learning) involves both grammatical competence and pragmatic competence (performance) and they occur simultaneously.</li>\n<li>The distinction between Competence (I-language) and Performance (E-language) at the level of learning is not tenable.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p><em>Simultaneously</em> means at the same time; <em>not tenable</em> means it cannot be defended. So when we <strong>learn</strong> a language, its grammar and its use cannot be kept apart: “the two needs to come together, only then we learn the target language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>). The slide “Proposal” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=505s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:25</a>) adds that the “evidence comes from data from natural language”: the lecture uses sentences from English and Hindi, and asks you to compare them with the languages you speak (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"learning-a-language-is-learning-to-learn\">Learning a language is learning to learn</h3>\n<p>The slide “Language is the ontogenesis of learning” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Halliday (1993) points out ‘when children learn language they are not simply engaging in one kind of learning among many; rather they are learning the foundation of learning itself. … Hence the ontogenesis of language is at the same time the ontogenesis of learning (p.93)’.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Ontogenesis</strong> means how something grows and develops in one person, from birth on. M.A.K. Halliday studied language from the side of learning, acquisition and education. We learn many things in life: to walk, swim, cycle, drive, cook, dance and sing. Learning a language is “not just one among all other kinds of learning”: it is “learning about learning itself” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide “Language and Linguistics” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=730s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:10</a>) quotes Mark Crimmins (<em>Routledge Encyclopedia of Philosophy</em>): “Language is an impressive and fascinating human capacity, and human languages are strikingly powerful and complex systems.” <strong>Linguistics</strong> is the systematic study of language. Because languages are so complex, “it is not possible to learn the underlying structural nuances by practicing, parroting, that is, practicing like your parrot” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). (A <strong>nuance</strong> is a small, fine difference.) What works, for a child or an adult, is to understand the patterns under the language: “there is no alternative to this” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A slide shown for a moment</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Just before Halliday, a slide quotes the anthropologist Shirley Brice Heath (1984): “Language increasingly will be a natural part of the research domains of fields ranging from computer science to industrial sociology. In the days ahead the foundational knowledge about language that has come from linguistics will be like certain principles of mathematics, physics, and biology.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:20</a>; the lecturer moves on without discussing it.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"speaking-is-instinctive-writing-is-learnt\">Speaking is instinctive; writing is learnt</h3>\n<p>The slide “Language: An Innate Endowment” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=855s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:15</a>). (<strong>Innate</strong>: present from birth; an <strong>endowment</strong> is a gift.)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Man has instinctive tendency to speak as we see in the babbles of our children while no child has an instinctive tendency to bake, brew, and write.” - Charles Darwin</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Instinctive</strong> means we are born with it. It belongs to the mind (<strong>cognition</strong>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Babble</strong> is the sound a baby makes before real words. Even babbling tells us the child is going to speak (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Baking, brewing (making coffee) and writing are things a child must be taught: “very carefully picked up activities”. So “learning to speak is different from learning to write” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Of the four skills, <strong>LSRW</strong> (listening, speaking, reading and writing), reading and writing go together, but speaking is very different from writing. Thousands of people speak a language and do not write it; “it never happens that you know how to write, but you do not speak the language”. “Human beings are genetically designed to speak natural language, whereas we need to learn additionally to write” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The same slide: “Language is one of the most sophisticated activities and human mind. The development of this capacity requires input from immediate society.” <strong>Input</strong> is the language we hear around us. “The more input we provide through our ears in our mind, the processing becomes faster” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"chomsky-from-habits-to-a-generative-mind\">Chomsky: from habits to a generative mind</h3>\n<p>For a long time, learning a language was thought to be <strong>stimulus and response</strong>: a “pattern drill”, like hearing something and repeating it until it becomes a habit (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). (A <strong>stimulus</strong> is what we receive; the <strong>response</strong> is what we give back.) From 1957, the work of Noam Chomsky changed this view. The slide lists three <strong>epistemological</strong> questions that come out of his work, that is, questions about knowledge: what it is and how we get it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). (Chomsky put the three questions in this form later, in 1986; 1957 is the year of his first book, <em>Syntactic Structures</em>.) The slide “Chomsky’s epistemological questions” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1205s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:05</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What constitutes knowledge of language?</li>\n<li>How is knowledge of language acquired?</li>\n<li>How is this knowledge of language put in practice?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Chomsky asked them about young children, who speak the language of their society by four or five years of age (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>). The course takes from this what helps adults, and the third question, how the knowledge is put into practice, applies to adults too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide “Revolution in understanding language learning” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1455s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:15</a>), which the lecturer crosses out in its first half:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rejected</strong>: Stimulus = Response; Input = Output; “Language Learning is a matter of habit formation”. Language learning “does not work merely through practice” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Instead</strong>: the <strong>poverty of stimulus</strong>: “Imperfect/fuzzy stimulus yields perfect learning”. And “Language computation is part of generative mechanism of human mind”: the psychologists missed that the computation happens in the mind (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>generative mechanism</strong> is the mind’s ability to produce (generate) new sentences from rules. This is why the course keeps asking you to listen to the news and read for half an hour a day: it is <strong>input</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>). That input is <strong>imperfect</strong> (“not directed towards learning”) and <strong>fuzzy</strong> (“not very clear”), yet for children it yields perfect learning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Knowledge of language (KoL) (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1605s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:45</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“KoL consists of underlying rules that we know but we do not know that we know them.” These rules trigger the generative mechanism of a speaker, so that speakers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“produce sentences that they may not have heard before”, and</li>\n<li>can “separate grammatical sentences from ungrammatical ones”.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Children do it without noticing; adults must notice</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Children derive the rules of their first language through a <strong>subconscious</strong> process. Adults learning English “need to do it through conscious process”: we must <strong>uncover</strong> the rules and learn them knowingly (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>). That is why this course explains patterns instead of asking you to memorise.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"context-is-built-into-a-language\">Context is built into a language</h3>\n<p>The slide title: “Learning language includes acquisition of in-built socio-cultural context” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:40</a>). (<strong>Socio-cultural</strong>: belonging to a society and its culture.) The lecture uses Hindi “only as an example”, written in Roman letters, and you can check every point with any Hindi speaker (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>). Each example has three lines: the Hindi sentence, its word-by-word meaning, and the English sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2940s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 49:00</a>). A star * marks a sentence that is not grammatical (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</p>\n<p>Hindi uses <strong>postpositions</strong>: small words such as <em>ke</em>, <em>ke paas</em> and <em>ko</em> that come <strong>after</strong> a noun, where English puts a preposition <strong>before</strong> it.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Raju has two kids; Raju has two cars (the lecture’s sentences 1 and 2 at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>raajuu ke do bacce haiN</em> (Raju poss two kids are): ‘Raju has two kids.’ Good. (<em>poss</em> = the possessive marker <em>ke</em>.)</li>\n<li>*<em>raajuu ke paas do bacce haiN</em>: not grammatical. <em>Ke paas</em> literally means “near”, in the sense of belonging (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>raajuu ke paas do gaariyaaN haiN</em> (Raju poss two cars are): ‘Raju has two cars.’ Good (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>). With <em>kii</em> in place of <em>ke paas</em>, the slide stars it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</li>\n<li>English uses the same structure, with <em>has</em>, in both sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> in Hindi, <em>what</em> Raju has (children or cars) changes the grammar; in English it does not. English speakers also know that kids and cars are different, but “this distinction is made in human cognition, but not in this structure” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Raju wants a pen (the lecture’s sentences 3 and 4 at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>raajuu kalam caahtaa hai</em> (Raju pen wants is): ‘Raju wants a pen.’ The slide marks it */? (odd). “There is nothing ungrammatical about sentence number three. But this looks odd” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2310s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:30</a>); “nobody speaks like this” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>).</li>\n<li>What Hindi speakers say: <em>raajuu ko kalam caahiye</em> (Raju to pen need): ‘Raju needs/wants a pen’ (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Caahtaa hai</em> is fine with an action: <em>raajuu jaanaa caahtaa hai</em> (wants to go), and so with <em>khaanaa</em> (to eat), <em>piinaa</em> (to drink), <em>dekhnaa</em> (to see), and <em>gaaRii khariidnaa caahtaa hai</em> (wants to buy a car). It sounds odd only with a thing: a pen, clothes, money or a car (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2340s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 39:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 41:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2490s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 41:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Caahiye</em> is a “frozen verb”: it does not change with the tense or with the subject (<em>Raju ko</em>, <em>Sarita ko</em>, <em>Rani ko</em>: the same verb). The postposition <em>ko</em> after the name makes the sentence indirect (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2550s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 42:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2580s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 43:00</a>).</li>\n<li>English allows both, with different meanings: <em>Raju wants a pen</em> and <em>Raju needs a pen</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2640s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 44:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Hindi turns a wish for a worldly object into a need. The lecturer’s explanation, which he invites you to check: Hindi speakers “do not want to verbalize their desire” for things in direct sentences, so “we want to say, I want, but we convert it into need” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2520s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 42:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2610s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 43:30</a>). English has no such rule: <em>Raju wants money</em> is perfectly normal.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A footnote: <em>drink</em> with no object</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>With no object, Hindi <em>piinaa</em> (to drink) means drinking alcohol: <em>raajuu piinaa caahtaa hai</em> means Raju wants to drink alcohol. English works the same way: “Raju wants to drink” suggests alcohol unless you say what he wants to drink: juice, water or milk (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2400s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 40:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 41:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"request-or-order-the-context-decides\">Request or order? The context decides</h3>\n<p>An <strong>imperative</strong> is a sentence that asks someone to do something (Week 3: <a href=\"#/english2/w03/imperatives-have-a-hidden-subject\">imperatives have a hidden subject</a>). The slide “Ambiguities: Imperatives” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2725s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 45:25</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Hindi (word by word)</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>Style</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><em>ghar aa-o</em> (home come-imp)</td>\n<td>‘Come home.’</td>\n<td>informal (subject <em>tum</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><em>ghar aa-iye</em> (home come-imp)</td>\n<td>‘Please come home.’</td>\n<td>formal (subject <em>aap</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<em>imp</em> = imperative.) The difference is <strong>informal versus formal</strong>, not order versus request: “five is not necessarily an order and six is not necessarily a request” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2820s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 47:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Ghar aao</em> said to a friend is “a quite decent invitation”, not an order (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2760s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 46:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A boss who says the formal <em>kal subah miliye</em> (“please see me tomorrow morning”) to a junior employee is not making a request: “for that employee, it is an order” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2820s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 47:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2850s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 47:30</a>).</li>\n<li>English works the same way. “Please see me tomorrow in the morning” said to an employee “does not give an option to that employee”; “see me tomorrow” said to a friend “is not an order” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2880s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 48:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“Learning about the context happens simultaneously with learning about structural nuances” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2910s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 48:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"saying-no-without-saying-no\">Saying no without saying no</h3>\n<p>The slide “Negation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2935s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 48:55</a>). (In the word-by-word lines, <em>erg</em> marks the subject marker <em>-ne</em>, <em>fut</em> means future tense, and <em>Imp</em> is what the sentence implies.)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ol start=\"7\">\n<li><em>aap-ne kahaa aap aais-kriim khariidenge</em> (you-erg said you ice-cream buy-fut): ‘You said you will buy ice-cream.’</li>\n<li><em>maiN-ne kab kahaa</em> (I-erg when said): ‘When did I say?’ Imp: I did not say so.</li>\n<li><em>maiN-ne kahaaN kahaa</em> (I-erg where said): ‘Where did I say?’ Imp: I did not say so.</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Sentence 7 builds the context (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2970s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 49:30</a>). Sentences 8 and 9 are <strong>questions</strong> in form, but they mean “I did not say I will buy ice-cream” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3000s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 50:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3090s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 51:30</a>). We do not want to <strong>refute</strong> the other person’s expectation directly (to refute: to say plainly that something is wrong), so we turn the denial into a question (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3060s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 51:00</a>). “To imply what we want to say sometimes we use a different structure, namely questions” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3090s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 51:30</a>). To <strong>imply</strong> is to mean something without saying it in words.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“I have to go shopping” (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 52:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Your friend says: “Let us go watch a movie today.”</li>\n<li>You reply: “I have to go shopping.”</li>\n<li>The friend may not care about your shopping: your reply is not really information about shopping (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 52:30</a>).</li>\n<li>From the context, the friend understands one thing very clearly: the answer is <em>no</em>, “and nobody makes this mistake” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 53:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the reply means “No, I cannot go”. It is a marker of <strong>politeness</strong>: a plain <em>no</em> “sounds confrontational, negative”, so we give another piece of information and let the hearer read the meaning. “This is applicable in English too” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 53:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 53:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A slide on metaphoric expressions</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A slide shown briefly (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 48:50</a>): “Children i.e. early learners do not require structured instruction for developing an understanding of functional properties (pragmatic competence) of language. However, the adult learners in learning language require them specifically.” Its examples: <em>lakshman rekhaa</em>, <em>kick the bucket</em>, <em>khet kii mulii</em>. (My explanation: these are idioms, whose meaning is not the sum of their words. <em>Kick the bucket</em> means “to die”; a <em>lakshman rekhaa</em>, from the Ramayana, is a line one must not cross.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Accuracy in the structure is not enough</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every language has its own in-built socio-cultural context, and our first language “has lot of implications for our learning of a new language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>). Know where English differs from your language: English uses <em>has</em> for kids and cars alike, and <em>wants</em> for things as well as actions. “If we are aware of these distinctions, then we end up becoming a better learner, a better speaker” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s conclusion: building grammatical competence “has to come along with pragmatic competence and only then, we get successful in building communicative confidence for us in the target language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 53:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 54:00</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-an-essay-or-a-research-report\">Writing an essay or a research report</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.2 (Rajesh Kumar). “Writing is an art, writing is a learnable skill” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). As children, we learn to speak and listen automatically, without lessons: we only need to hear the language around us (<strong>input</strong>), and the mind’s <strong>language acquisition device</strong> (its built-in equipment for learning language) turns it into speech “in a miraculous way” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). Learning a new language later, “particularly after 15 years of age”, needs effort and systematic instruction (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). Writing is different again: it “is not an innate capacity”; it “can be learned only by efforts” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"listening-speaking-and-the-soft-skills\">Listening, speaking and the soft skills</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Listening comes first.</strong> “In order to be able to speak a new language the precondition is listening”: we must hear the language we want to speak (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Pragmatic constraints.</strong> Part of pragmatic competence is knowing “what to speak, what not to speak, more importantly, when to speak and when not to speak”. These are “softer skills”, part of normal etiquette (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). “At times, we say a lot by not saying anything”, and our <strong>body language</strong> (the movements of our body) says a lot too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Accuracy.</strong> “Without accuracy, we do not sound effective. Without accuracy, we are not taken seriously.” Accuracy builds confidence, and then you can put your ideas across “with more ease and effectiveness” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-writing-is\">What writing is</h3>\n<p>The slide “What is writing?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=50s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:50</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Writing is the art of expressing our views, ideas, and thoughts.</li>\n<li>Though writing seems easy while thinking about it, many times, it results in unorganized and scattered pieces of information on paper and creates confusion or sends a wrong message.</li>\n<li>Effective writing can be a daunting task as it demands much more than mere correct grammar.</li>\n<li>You need to gain knowledge about sentence structure, develop your vocabulary, and build other basic writing skills.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>(<em>Daunting</em>: so hard that it can frighten you.) How writing differs from speaking:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It is learnt.</strong> Writing “can be learned only by efforts” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>In a first language</strong>, millions of people speak flawlessly but cannot write, because they never learnt: speakers of Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Odia, Bangla, Assamese and many more (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>In a second language</strong> such as English, the opposite is usual: when we learn a language later, “writing precedes speaking” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>The reader is alone with the page.</strong> This is the “most fundamental” point: “when we communicate through writing, we are not there to speak those things”. The reader understands only “what is written on the piece of paper”, and we “do not get a chance to repeat ourselves ... to explain ourselves” once we have finished writing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"make-writing-a-habit\">Make writing a habit</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Write every day.</strong> “Writing is also a matter of habit formation.” It is “self-indulgent”: you invest yourself in it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). It gives relief from what is going on in your mind and around you, because it captures your attention and helps you focus: “writing is like yoga” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Think first.</strong> “You need to think, ideate, stop, meditate a lot before you can put things in writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). At first your ideas may look disorganised and scattered on paper; thinking a point through clearly shows on the paper too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Paper or screen.</strong> A computer makes it easy to rearrange sentences, and software can check them. But writing in a notebook, though it “may sound old fashioned”, is “undoubtedly more helpful”, because then the checking happens in your own mind (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Do not fear the record.</strong> We hesitate to write because writing creates a document, “some point from where we do not go back” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Read carefully.</strong> Your vocabulary, sentence structure, punctuation and knowledge of a subject “are going to get imported from your time that you invest in reading” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The 90-day test</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Start writing on a piece of paper, give yourself 90 days.” Then compare day 90 with day 1: you will hardly recognise your own writing, and many grammatical mistakes will have disappeared. “If you make mistakes, leave it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Habit or not? (my note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.1 said that learning a language is <strong>not</strong> a matter of habit formation; here writing <strong>is</strong> “a matter of habit formation”. There is no contradiction: speaking grows from a capacity we are born with, while writing is a learnt skill that grows with practice.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"steps-to-write-well\">Steps to write well</h3>\n<p>The slides “Writing made simple: Steps to write well” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:50</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1435s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:55</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The slide</th>\n<th>What the lecture says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Give your writing structure</td>\n<td>Have clear points in a particular order, as for an official letter (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Make sure you’re clear on the concepts you’re writing about</td>\n<td>Clarity about the concept comes before writing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>If the message is complex, outline it</td>\n<td>“Outline things in order to reduce complexity” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anticipate your readers’ questions</td>\n<td>Answer in your writing the questions a reader may have afterwards (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Don’t over-explain everything</td>\n<td>Strike a balance: do not underestimate your readers, and do not leave too much to their imagination (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Go easy on the prepositional phrases</td>\n<td>Avoid long sentences overloaded with phrases; be careful with subordinate clauses too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eliminate the filler words and phrases</td>\n<td>Leave out the fillers of speech: <em>ah, umm, I think so, I do not know</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Don’t pad weak words with adverbs; stick with simple words; use contractions</td>\n<td>(on the slide only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Practice, practice, practice!</td>\n<td>“You will need to practice to get to that point” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pack your writing like a travel bag (the lecture’s picture at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>When we pack for a trip, the things we need later go at the bottom.</li>\n<li>The things we need on the way, or just after arriving, go on top.</li>\n<li>Writing needs “that type of preparation”: decide what the reader needs first and what can come later.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> an outline is the packing plan of your writing. It reduces the complexity before you begin.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Prepositional phrase and adjunct</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>prepositional phrase</strong> begins with a preposition: <em>in the night</em>, <em>with his friends</em>, <em>after dinner</em>. Such phrases are usually <strong>adjuncts</strong>: optional parts of a sentence that add information (when, where, how). Week 2’s sentence <em>John reads a book in the night with his friends after dinner</em> has three of them. One or two are fine; a long chain makes a sentence heavy (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two slide points the lecture does not explain (my note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Don’t pad weak words with adverbs” means: choose one strong word instead of a weak word with <em>very</em> or <em>really</em> in front (<em>very tired</em> → <em>exhausted</em>; my example). “Use contractions” (<em>don’t</em>, <em>it’s</em>) makes writing sound natural, close to speech; in formal reports and applications many readers still prefer the full forms (<em>do not</em>, <em>it is</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"know-your-type-and-your-reader\">Know your type and your reader</h3>\n<p>“What is the type of our writing ... what is our goal? Who is our intended audience? If that is in mind, it becomes very helpful” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>). The slide “Essays and its types” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1495s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:55</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it does (my short explanations)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Argumentative essay</td>\n<td>argues for a position, with reasons and evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Admissions essay</td>\n<td>is written to get admission to a college or a programme, like the SOP of <a href=\"#/english2/w08/statement-of-purpose-what-it-is-for\">Week 8</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Persuasive essay</td>\n<td>tries to make the reader agree or act</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare and contrast essay</td>\n<td>shows how two things are alike and how they differ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personal essay</td>\n<td>tells your own experience, “in terms of a memoir or a diary” (the lecture)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expository essay</td>\n<td>explains a topic with facts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Each type has its own requirements. Clarity and accuracy are the foundations, but “you do not need to strive for clarity and accuracy right on the first day”: they come “through writing over a long period of time” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide “An Effective Essay” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1575s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:15</a>) gives eight steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Decide what kind of essay to write.</li>\n<li>Brainstorm your topic.</li>\n<li>Research the topic.</li>\n<li>Choose a writing style.</li>\n<li>Develop a thesis.</li>\n<li>Outline your essay.</li>\n<li>Write your essay.</li>\n<li>Edit your writing to check spelling and grammar.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(To <strong>brainstorm</strong> is to write down every idea that comes to mind, before choosing.) The lecturer’s comments: research the topic carefully; use the style and the vocabulary of that type of writing; make a short outline; and finally <strong>edit</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Re-read before you send</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Make it a habit that you reread, edit, and then send it to somebody”, above all an e-mail: “once you have sent it, there is nothing to undo”. “Rereading is the process of editing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"writing-a-research-report\">Writing a research report</h3>\n<p>A research report is “a very specific thing”: the research comes first, and the report puts what has already happened “in that order in writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). The order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>State the research problem</strong>, and describe it.</li>\n<li><strong>Describe the method</strong>: “the steps that we have taken in order to do research”.</li>\n<li><strong>The body</strong>: “present evidence, an argument in support of the evidence”.</li>\n<li><strong>The conclusion</strong>: “the conclusive points derived out of the research”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>All research is academic, so a report must also show familiarity with the topic: a <strong>literature survey</strong> or <strong>literature review</strong> (a summary of what others have already written on it), which “has helped you sharpen up your question”. “The key point in a research report is the research question”, and the report shows “the trajectory through which you have reached the question” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>). (A <em>trajectory</em> is a path.)</p>\n<p>The slide “Research Report” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>): “Research papers are intended to demonstrate a student’s academic knowledge of a subject.” There are three categories:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>If your paper …</th>\n<th>it is …</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>presents an analysis of information</td>\n<td>analytical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>explains information</td>\n<td>expository</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>argues a conclusion</td>\n<td>argumentative or persuasive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Research: a systematic process (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Research in other word means a systematic process”, and “anything that is systematic is scientific”. It has three stages, in this order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Observation</strong>: we first observe something.</li>\n<li><strong>Description</strong>: we describe what we observed.</li>\n<li><strong>Explanation</strong>: we explain what we observed and described.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>The order matters: it is “impossible to explain anything unless we have described it for ourselves. And how do we describe something if we have not observed carefully” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Observation, description, explanation (my example)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Observe</strong>: in one class, many students submit their weekly work late.</li>\n<li><strong>Describe</strong>: count it: how many students, in which weeks, how late.</li>\n<li><strong>Explain</strong>: only now ask why. Perhaps the late weeks are the weeks with a quiz in another course.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> an explanation is built on a careful description, and the description on careful observation. Skip a stage and the explanation is only a guess.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Because “in this entire process, it is impossible to remember everything”, we make notes, organise them and keep an outline in mind: how we introduce the topic, the body, and how we reach the conclusion (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>). The lecturer goes quickly through slides on this (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1964s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:44</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=2007s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:27</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Slide</th>\n<th>Its main points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Step One: Re-search</td>\n<td>Understand your subject; formulate ideas for your paper; develop a thesis statement; gather resource materials and begin reviewing them. Sources: Google Scholar; online encyclopedias, almanacs and databases; books and periodicals; newspapers; government publications, guides and reports.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Step Two: Make notes</td>\n<td>Take notes as you read; keep track of your references so that you can cite them and build your bibliography; try a spreadsheet with columns such as Title, Author, Reference link, Page number and Quotes. “The Purdue Online Writing Lab (OWL) and other university writing lab websites are excellent resources”.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Step Three: Organise</td>\n<td>“Don’t skip the organization step”: without it your paper “will lack focus”. Write a <strong>thesis statement</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The outline</td>\n<td>The main points that support the thesis become your sub-headings; organise your information under each; delete what does not fit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The introduction</td>\n<td>It answers three questions: <em>What is this? Why am I reading it? What do you want me to do?</em> Set the context, say why the main idea matters, state your thesis.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The body</td>\n<td>“Your outline isn’t meant to be a prison”; “Don’t let your sources organize your paper!”; consider the <strong>Rule of Three</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The conclusion</td>\n<td>Restate the thesis in new words; briefly summarise your points; explain why they support your case; if the research is inconclusive, say why the topic needs further research.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(A <strong>bibliography</strong> is the list of sources at the end of a paper; to <strong>cite</strong> a source is to name it where you use it.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Thesis statement (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1985s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:05</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A sentence that summarizes the main point of your essay and previews your supporting points.” It guides your readers from the beginning by telling them the main idea and the supporting points. “Your thesis statement should match the type of paper you’re writing.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Logos, pathos and ethos (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1995s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:15</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the introduction, state the position you will support with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>logos</strong>: sound reasoning (induction, deduction);</li>\n<li><strong>pathos</strong>: a balanced emotional appeal;</li>\n<li><strong>ethos</strong>: the author’s credibility (why the reader can trust you).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The Rule of Three (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=2001s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:21</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Find supporting arguments for each point you make.</li>\n<li>Present a strong point first, followed by an even stronger one.</li>\n<li>Finish with your strongest point.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Checklist for Revision” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=2007s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:27</a>), to go through before you hand in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is your thesis statement clear and concise?</li>\n<li>Is your paper well-organized, and does it flow from beginning to end with logical transitions?</li>\n<li>Do your ideas follow a logical sequence in each paragraph?</li>\n<li>Have you used concrete details and facts and avoided generalizations?</li>\n<li>Do your arguments support and prove your thesis?</li>\n<li>Have you avoided repetition?</li>\n<li>Are your sources properly cited?</li>\n<li>Have you checked for accidental plagiarism?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<strong>Plagiarism</strong> is using someone else’s words or ideas as if they were your own.) “The better the report the better you have a possibility to convince others” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"impressive-is-not-ornamental\">Impressive is not ornamental</h3>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Too many adjectives, too many passives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Impressive does not mean selecting too many adjectives or too many adverbs. In fact, we should avoid using too many adjectives. We should avoid writing too many passive sentences.” Impact comes from organising the ideas properly in your mind (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>). (Week 8’s <a href=\"#/english2/w08/writing-a-lab-report\">lab report</a> uses the passive on purpose, because there the doer does not matter. The warning here is against <em>too many</em>.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“For the reader, we are only present in the form of our words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=2130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:30</a>). “Nobody becomes perfect, right in the beginning ... if it takes time, it is not abnormal.” The last advice: “please continue writing. Indulge yourself in writing. You will get there” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>). Week 9’s <a href=\"#/english2/w09/developing-writing-skills\">writing lectures</a> give two step-by-step methods, CODER and POWER, for the same journey.</p>\n<h2 id=\"decent-e-mails\">Decent e-mails</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.3 (Rajesh Kumar). We all write e-mails every day; some people write hundreds. So what is there to learn? “Certain very obvious things”, which we still see broken every day (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). By a <strong>decent</strong> e-mail the lecturer means “a neatly designed and good-looking, short, crisp writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>): an e-mail that looks good and does its job “by putting the desired impact” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). (<em>Crisp</em>: short and clear; <em>succinct</em>: saying a lot in few words.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>An <strong>e-mail</strong> (electronic mail) is a letter sent electronically from one machine to another, “in a personalised fashion” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). It is a form of writing, so the features of writing apply. The slide “Writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=255s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:15</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Permanent</strong>: once someone has seen it, we cannot change it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence</strong>: “it can become a piece of evidence, once we write, we cannot take it back” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Time at disposal</strong>: unlike speech, which flows, writing lets us take as much time as we need (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Rewriting is possible</strong>: “several rounds of rewriting” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Grammaticality is extremely important</strong>: “we cannot afford to be ungrammatical in writing”, nor in speaking (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Must think about achieving goals</strong>: “every piece of email has a specific goal and we must not miss that aim” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide “Types of Emails” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=615s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:15</a>): “only three types”: <strong>professional</strong>, <strong>personal</strong>, and “somewhere in the middle” (semi-professional or semi-personal). The two rules, aim and grammaticality, apply to all three (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). The slide “Purpose of an Email” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=695s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:35</a>): “Fast communication of information”. Because it is fast, it needs <strong>caution</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-parts-of-an-e-mail\">The parts of an e-mail</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s model e-mail (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:20</a>; “these are just fictitious names”, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>From:</strong> Samuel Allison &lt;samuel.allison@xyz-inc.com&gt;<br>\n<strong>To:</strong> Karen Jones &lt;karen.jones@lmno-inc.com&gt;<br>\n<strong>Subject:</strong> Marks report</p>\n<p>Dear Karen:</p>\n<p>Have you completed a revision of the Marks report, and if so, may I have a copy of it? I would like to take it to my meeting with Rachel tomorrow.</p>\n<p>I will be in my office until noon if you have any questions.</p>\n<p>Regards,<br>\nSam</p>\n<p>Samuel Allison<br>\nDirector, Marketing<br>\nXYZ, Inc.<br>\n123 Anywhere Street<br>\nNew York, NY 10024<br>\nTel: (212) 555-1234<br>\nFax: (212) 555-3456<br>\nsamuel.allison@xyz-inc.com<br>\nwww.xyz-inc.com</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>What the lecture says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>From</strong></td>\n<td>Set up your e-mail program so that your mail arrives listed under your <strong>name</strong>, not just your address (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>To</strong></td>\n<td>Write the recipient’s name and address properly, as <em>Karen Jones &lt;karen.jones@…&gt;</em>: it shows you made the effort (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Subject</strong></td>\n<td>Never empty and never “just anything”: “this is as important as the body of the text” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Salutation</strong></td>\n<td>Always address the recipient: <em>Dear Karen:</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Message content</strong></td>\n<td>Get to the point and be precise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Closing</strong></td>\n<td>A closing line or two, then <em>Regards,</em> and your name (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Signature file</strong></td>\n<td>Your name, position and workplace; address, phone, e-mail and website (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The <strong>body</strong> is everything from the salutation to the signature.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">An empty or careless subject line</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The recipient first sees two things: <strong>who</strong> sent the e-mail and <strong>what it is about</strong>. That is “the first impression” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). Many e-mail programs filter mail: an e-mail without a proper subject, or with an absurd one, can land in the junk folder or the trash and never reach the reader. “It is your responsibility” that it is delivered and that the recipient feels like opening it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A succinct body (the lecture’s reading at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Have you completed a revision of the Marks report?</em> The question comes at once, with no small talk.</li>\n<li><em>If so, may I have a copy of it?</em> The request.</li>\n<li><em>I would like to take it to my meeting with Rachel tomorrow.</em> The reason for the request.</li>\n<li>Nothing else is added (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> this is a <strong>succinct</strong> message: it asks for something, gives the reason, “and it has no additional information”. It seems written to a colleague or to office staff whom the writer meets daily, so <em>How are you?</em> is not needed (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>If you write after a long time, one sentence is enough (<em>How have you been? It has been a while since we spoke</em>); “after a sentence or two, you immediately get back to the main point” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>). The signature should not carry “too many unnecessary details, either too many graphics that may or may not open in other clients” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"subject-lines-and-salutations\">Subject lines and salutations</h3>\n<p>The slide “Subject and Salutation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1520s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:20</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subject</strong>: <em>Reg. Sick Leave</em>, <em>Reg. Resignation</em> (<em>Reg.</em> = regarding). Give the topic of the mail, and capitalise every word except articles and prepositions.</li>\n<li><strong>Salutation</strong>: <em>Hello</em> (formal; capitalise the first word and all nouns: <em>Hello Sir</em>), <em>Dear</em> (formal), <em>Hi</em> (neutral), <em>Hey</em> (informal).</li>\n<li>Use a <strong>colon or a comma</strong> after the salutation: the colon is American English (AmE), the comma British English (BrE). The model e-mail has the American <em>Dear Karen:</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecturer adds that <em>Hey</em> is “very informal”: use it only in “a very informal context” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>). When you do not know the person, <em>Dear Sir</em> or <em>Dear Ma’am</em> “is the safest way” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>). Week 9 put the comma after a salutation among the <a href=\"#/english2/w09/words-outside-the-main-sentence\">commas for words outside the main sentence</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Titles go with the last name (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In English, a title (<em>Mr, Ms, Dr, Professor</em>) is followed by the person’s <strong>last name</strong>. Without a title, use the <strong>first name</strong> alone.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>✓ <em>Dear Karen</em>, <em>Dear Dr Jones</em>, <em>Dear Mr Jones</em></li>\n<li>✗ <em>Dear Mr Karen</em>, <em>Ms Karen</em>, <em>Dr Karen</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Our own naming systems</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“In our country, in several contexts, several cultures, we have different ways of using salutations. We have different systems of our names. So, it is okay for our system and our culture; you do not need to change. But when you are writing something in English, then you must use the last name with the title or just the first name” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"useful-phrases\">Useful phrases</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s slides give ready phrases for each job (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1575s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:15</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1655s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:35</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Job</th>\n<th>Phrases (from the slides)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Introducing a topic or informing</td>\n<td><em>This is to inform you that …</em> (formal); <em>Just to let you know that …</em> (informal); <em>Good news! / Bad news!</em> and <em>I have got good/bad news!</em> (neutral)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Following up on a previous discussion</td>\n<td><em>As we discussed …</em>; <em>To follow up on our meeting or discussion …</em>; <em>Regarding / in regards to …</em> (careful writers prefer <em>in regard to</em> or <em>with regard to</em>); <em>As far as … goes …</em>; <em>On the topic of …</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Asking and requesting</td>\n<td><em>I would like to know if / when / how …</em>; <em>Would you let me know when / if / how …</em>; <em>Could you confirm if / when / how …</em>; <em>Do you know if / when / how …</em>; <em>Do you have any details or updates on …</em>; <em>Could you give me an update / a quote / an estimate …</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Saying thanks</td>\n<td><em>Thanks for</em> getting back to me / the information / the heads up / the update / the email / following up on … / your help with … / looking into this.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ending the mail</td>\n<td><em>I will get back to you as soon as I can.</em> <em>I will let you know.</em> <em>I will keep you posted.</em> <em>(Please/Kindly) let me know.</em> <em>(Please/Kindly) keep me posted.</em> <em>Thanks / Regards / All the best</em>; <em>Best regards</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(A <em>heads up</em> is an early warning; to <em>keep someone posted</em> is to keep them informed.) In a closing, capitalise the first word only: <em>Best regards</em>, not <em>Best Regards</em> (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1655s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:35</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Say thank you in words</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“There is a difference between our languages and English. In our languages a lot of gratitude can be unspoken as well. However, in English, we must be explicit about these things” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"spacing\">Spacing</h3>\n<p>The slide “Spacing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:40</a>), with the model e-mail beside it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leave an empty line after the salutation.</li>\n<li>Leave an empty line between paragraphs.</li>\n<li>Leave an empty line before the closing.</li>\n<li>Leave an empty line after the closing.</li>\n<li>No empty line or comma after the sign.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the model, <em>Regards,</em> is followed by the name <em>Sam</em>, with no comma after <em>Sam</em>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-before-you-press-send\">Rules before you press Send</h3>\n<p>The slide “Some crucial aspects” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1670s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:50</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not reply right away except when it is required.</li>\n<li>Do not reply when you are angry.</li>\n<li>Do proofread what you write.</li>\n<li>In fact, do read it at least three times.</li>\n<li>Do not forward to a large group.</li>\n<li>Do write a subject line.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecturer’s explanations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Take time.</strong> Unless a reply is needed at once, “take some time to think about what is being asked for”. If an e-mail disturbs you, give yourself time to calm down, think, and then respond (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Read and proofread.</strong> “Reading is for checking your content, proofreading is checking your grammaticality.” Read it three times, twice at the very least: “it sounds too much, but not really” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Forwarding.</strong> “People usually do not like forwarded emails, unless it is important for them to know about why the email is being forwarded.” Give the context in a line of your own (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Mass e-mails.</strong> Take extra care. When you reply or forward, change the subject line if needed, so that it stays clear. “People, in general, do not respond to mass emails”: if you need a reply from someone, write to them individually (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“People should look forward to receiving something from you. People should not delete, people should not junk your mails” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"barriers-to-communication\">Barriers to communication</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.4 (Om Prakash) opens with George Bernard Shaw: “the greatest illusion about communication is that it has taken place” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). (The line is popularly credited to Shaw, but it has not been found in his writings.) We send a message and believe the job is done. But did the message get across the way we meant it? Can the receiver decode it as we intended? “Despite all your honest and earnest efforts, the message may be misinterpreted, there may be distortion” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Knowing English, its grammar and its words is important; it is “equally important to have the skills to use them appropriately in an appropriate socio-cultural context” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Barriers to communication (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:20</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Barriers to Communication imply hurdles or obstacles on the way of transmission of the message from the sender to the receiver.” They cannot all be listed, but they fall into five categories:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Physical and mechanical barriers</li>\n<li>Language or semantic barriers</li>\n<li>Socio-psychological barriers</li>\n<li>Organizational barriers</li>\n<li>Personal barriers</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>If we know the barriers, we can reduce their impact: “we can minimize the degree of subjectivity and we can be objective and communicate effectively” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-communication-cycle\">The communication cycle</h3>\n<p>The process looks simple (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>sender</strong> <strong>encodes</strong> the message: puts it into a code, for example English.</li>\n<li>The <strong>receiver</strong> <strong>decodes</strong> it: turns the code back into meaning.</li>\n<li>The receiver encodes <strong>feedback</strong> (a response).</li>\n<li>The sender decodes the feedback, and the cycle is complete.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>“But a lot of other things happen between sending and receiving, between encoding and decoding” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). A message works when it has the right <strong>attributes</strong>: brevity (a small size), clarity, objectivity (no ambiguity, no room for open interpretation), simplicity, intelligibility (fitted to the level of your audience) and utility (useful to the receiver). If the reader “does not derive any value”, the message is ignored (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). Week 9 gave the same list as the <a href=\"#/english2/w09/attributes-of-an-effective-communicator\">attributes of an effective communicator</a>.</p>\n<p>“It does not come to you so quickly by mugging up and memorizing thousands of new words, low-frequency words by sounding intellectual by using complex sentences; it does not work.” What works is connecting with your audience, so that “you both are at an equal plane” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-physical-and-mechanical-barriers\">1. Physical and mechanical barriers</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>): “Noise is the disruption or interference in communication cycle. Noise can be physical as well as psychological.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Physical noise</strong>: gossip, loudspeakers, the noise of equipment or machines.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychological noise</strong>: mental disturbances such as a clash of egos, a preoccupied mind, a hangover, fatigue, anxiety, prejudices and strong negative emotions.</li>\n<li>Other attributes: <strong>time</strong> (the timing of the message), <strong>distance</strong> between the partners, <strong>external noise</strong>, <strong>any physical disturbance</strong>, and <strong>information overload</strong> (the pace of delivery against the pace of reception).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Speaker and listener often cannot control these barriers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). <strong>Information overload</strong> means sending more than the audience can take in. “It does not matter how intellectual you sound ... If your message is not interpreted by your audience ... then message has no value” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">You-oriented communication (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Communication is you oriented ... listener oriented, audience oriented, not I oriented.” What I want to say matters, but what matters more is whether my audience receives it properly. So the receiver is “the central point”, and you customise the message to the receiver’s level (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"2-language-or-semantic-barriers\">2. Language or semantic barriers</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>): “Language is the carrier of all verbal communication. As each one of us has got a different worldview and perception, the role of language becomes more imperative and significant in exchanging and sharing information. Lack of coherence, errors of omission, selection of improper words and technical jargon may mar the meaning and message of the communication.” Other attributes that create a barrier: unclear message, bad translation, technical jargon, unspecified assumptions, unclear connotation, metaphors, linguistic inappropriateness and socio-cultural inappropriateness.</p>\n<p>(<strong>Semantic</strong>: to do with meaning. A <strong>connotation</strong> is the extra feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning. <em>To mar</em>: to spoil.)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One language, many Englishes.</strong> English has broadly the same grammar and words in India, Canada, Britain, America and the Caribbean, but the speakers and their cultures differ. “Language as a code may be similar or same but the functions of these linguistic items are culture-specific.” Indian English structures “can be ambiguous and confusing for a British receiver” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Translation</strong> is “the most challenging thing”: an expression may have no equivalent, or lose its essence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions, connotations and context</strong> must be clear, and metaphors used appropriately (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Tone and stress.</strong> A wrong word, sentence, tone or stress can change the meaning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Social use.</strong> Greetings, politeness norms, starting a conversation, interrupting, intervening and disagreeing differ between Indian, British and American culture (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Time is money (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>English says: <em>Time is money.</em></li>\n<li>The Hindi saying is <em>Samay balwan hai</em>: “time is all powerful” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n<li>English speakers look at time in terms of money; the Hindi saying sees time as a power above us.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the gap is not in the words but in beliefs and values: “it is a socio-cultural gap”. Metaphors are “frozen expressions”, and “translating metaphors is a big challenge” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Why don’t you sit?” (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Someone visits you, and you say: <em>Why don’t you sit?</em></li>\n<li>In structure, it is a question.</li>\n<li>In use (its pragmatic meaning), it is an offer: you are offering a seat.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the meaning lies in the situation, not in the form. This is the pragmatic competence of <a href=\"#saying-no-without-saying-no\">Lecture 12.1</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"3-socio-psychological-barriers\">3. Socio-psychological barriers</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1485s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:45</a>): “Every individual perceives things differently. Given this inter-personal differences and individuality, receiving, interpreting, and decoding depend upon the psychological state of individual’s mind. The external environment plays a significant role in shaping of our individual as well as collective perception.” The socio-cultural differences are in terms of <strong>values</strong>, <strong>beliefs</strong>, <strong>stereotypes</strong>, <strong>group norms</strong>, <strong>bias</strong> and <strong>ethnocentrism</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>stereotype</strong> is a fixed, oversimplified idea about a group of people. We have stereotypes about gender, caste, class, region and religion, and they make us judgmental (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A <strong>group norm</strong> is a belief or practice that we keep “in order to remain the member of a group” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</li>\n<li>A <strong>bias</strong> is a leaning for or against someone. We have individual biases (our own likes and dislikes) and collective, social biases (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Ethnocentrism</strong> is judging other cultures by the standards of your own and seeing your own group as the best (definition mine; the lecture names it).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If we do not “work them out”, our judgments and our encoding of the message will be biased. “We need to be balanced and neutral” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-organizational-barriers\">4. Organizational barriers</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>): “An organization is a deliberate creation for attainment of specified objectives.” Its policies, rules, procedures, norms of behaviour and reward-and-punishment systems affect all its inner processes, “including communication in different directions”. The major organizational barriers: status, one-way flow, organizational hierarchy, rules and regulations, lack of compliance, different priorities, rigidity in policy, attitude towards seniors, and lack of trust in subordinates.</p>\n<p>“We create rules, regulations, networks to facilitate communication”, but they “also turn out to be a barrier” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>). “Your message is interpreted with relation to your location ... in a social space”: your status and your position in the organisation. Insisting on the “proper channel”, personal goals that differ from the organisation’s, rigid policies, and a deficit of trust between seniors and subordinates all block the free flow of information (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>). (A <strong>hierarchy</strong> is a system of ranks, from top to bottom; a <strong>subordinate</strong> is someone of lower rank.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-personal-barriers\">5. Personal barriers</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>) lists the inter-personal barriers “of special mention”: differences in perception, differences in attitudes, inattention, occupied mind, premature evaluation, resistance to change, selective omission, filtering of information, and message overload.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Premature evaluation</strong>: judging before we see the whole picture, because we are “desperate to speak” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Selective omission</strong>: “things that do not suit us we simply do not register” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Filtering</strong>: “we are not robots”: we filter, contextualise and interpret everything we receive (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cure is personal too: promptness, a proactive approach, attention, patience, being a good listener, not judging too early, and willingness to take part (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Barrier: where it comes from</th>\n<th>Examples from the slides</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Physical and mechanical</strong>: the surroundings and the channel</td>\n<td>noise, distance, timing, information overload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Language or semantic</strong>: words and their meanings</td>\n<td>bad translation, jargon, metaphors, unclear connotation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Socio-psychological</strong>: social background and the mind</td>\n<td>stereotypes, bias, group norms, ethnocentrism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Organizational</strong>: the structure of the organisation</td>\n<td>status, hierarchy, one-way flow, rigid rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Personal</strong>: the individual</td>\n<td>inattention, premature evaluation, selective omission</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"building-good-communication\">Building good communication</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:10</a>): “Developing and maintaining a system of good communication is the primary job of any executive. Effectiveness of Communication largely depends on reciprocal understanding, mutual exchange of ideas, facts, and information.” (<em>Reciprocal</em>: going both ways.)</p>\n<p>The slide “The characteristics and attributes of a good communication system” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Two-way channel</strong>: communication is participative; give your listener space to take part. “Turn taking is important, equally important is turn giving” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Mutual trust</strong>: connect with the audience and build trust (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Clarity of message</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Timely message</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Consistency of message</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Good reasoning</strong>: well-reasoned, logical messages.</li>\n<li><strong>Proactive relations</strong>; the lecturer adds: be positive, or at least express a positive outlook (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Periodical feedback</strong>: keep checking that the receiver is receiving (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Empathic listening</strong> (the slide spells it “Emphatic”): “you have to be very good listener to be a very good communicator” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>). Empathic listening means listening with feeling for the speaker’s point of view.</li>\n<li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: look at things in totality, “with open mind and open heart” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Socio-cultural appropriateness</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Communication is not gossip</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Communication is not about non-stop speaking, not about fluent speaking, not about appropriate accent and pronunciation; it is all about exchanging the meaningful content” without distortion and ambiguity. “There is a difference between gossip and communication”: communication has a goal, and “all our acts should converge towards achieving that communication goal” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2310s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2340s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 39:00</a>). Keep six questions in mind: <strong>who</strong> communicates <strong>what</strong> to <strong>whom</strong>, <strong>when</strong>, <strong>why</strong> and <strong>how</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=2280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"context-of-communication-the-speaking-model\">Context of communication: the SPEAKING model</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.5 (Om Prakash). When we say “I know English”, what do we mean? Words? Grammar rules? Pronunciation? Or “a total package”, which also includes understanding the situation in which all these are used (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>)? “Communication is all about the combination of form and function of the language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). The <strong>form</strong> is the shape of the language (words, sentences, sounds); the <strong>function</strong> is what it does in a real situation.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"from-linguistic-to-communicative-competence\">From linguistic to communicative competence</h3>\n<p>The sociolinguist Dell Hymes discussed the role of context in the mid-1960s and called his approach the <strong>ethnography of communication</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). (A <strong>sociolinguist</strong> studies language in society; <strong>ethnography</strong> is the close description of a people’s way of life.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Competence and performance (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Competence</strong>: the speaker’s <strong>tacit</strong> (unspoken, unconscious) knowledge of the language: the potential, the ability.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance</strong>: the actual act of using the language.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“You may be very competent, but it has to be reflected in your performance” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Chomsky’s <strong>linguistic competence</strong> is the computational side: a native speaker’s tacit knowledge of the structures, imagined in an <strong>ideal speaker and listener</strong>, untouched by memory loss or any other problem (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li>For Hymes this did not explain language well enough. Knowing a language also means using its rules “in appropriate socio-cultural context”. So he joined the two ideas: your performance reflects your competence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Communicative competence (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=430s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Dell Hymes coined the term Communicative Competence in 1966 as a response to the perceived inadequacy of Noam Chomsky’s (1965) distinction between Linguistic Competence and Linguistic Performance.” It puts “communicative form and function in integral relation to each other”: it is “the intuitive functional knowledge and control of the principles of language usage”. “Whether you are able to communicate properly or not, that determines your level of competence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“Language and culture are intertwined” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). When you meet a new group of people, you discover new words and also “a new set of cultural norms, communication patterns, their beliefs, conventions”: “language encodes culture” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>What it asks about</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Who speaks?</td>\n<td>the speaker, and the speaker’s place in the social space</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>To whom?</td>\n<td>the receiver, the audience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What?</td>\n<td>the message, the content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How?</td>\n<td>the manner: formal or informal, the sequence, the genre</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Why?</td>\n<td>the objective, the goal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When?</td>\n<td>the timing, the sequence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</p>\n<h3 id=\"formality-register-and-idiolect\">Formality, register and idiolect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Formality.</strong> We speak one way to seniors and elders, another way to juniors and colleagues: “the degree of formality” varies, and so does the choice of words and sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Register</strong>: the words, sentences and expressions of one domain. A meeting of doctors uses different technical words from a meeting of engineers, and both differ from an ordinary situation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Idiolect</strong>: each person’s own style of using language, “individual centric variations” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>The norms of a society.</strong> In some societies, overlapping speech or unwanted interruption is rude; in others, two or three people speak at once and communication goes on. How you open a conversation, how formal you are, how you agree, disagree and oppose: “it is purely cultural” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Interpretation.</strong> When people from different cultures talk, they interpret the same thing differently, so know the possible interpretations of your message (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three things we learn with every word (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Its <strong>spelling and pronunciation</strong> (spelling if we learn the word in writing, pronunciation if we learn it orally).</li>\n<li>Its <strong>meaning</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>context</strong> in which it is used.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>Week 9 called these the <a href=\"#/english2/w09/three-sides-of-every-word\">three sides of every word</a>. The first two are <strong>formal</strong> properties; the third is <strong>functional</strong>, and “this static knowledge of formal properties actually does not help. What helps, the combination of the two” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"sixteen-components-and-the-speaking-acronym\">Sixteen components and the SPEAKING acronym</h3>\n<p>The slide “The Sixteen Components Applied to Discourse” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=455s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:35</a>): message form; message content; setting; scene; speaker/sender; addressor; hearer/receiver/audience; addressee; purposes (outcomes); purposes (goals); key; channels; forms of speech; norms of interaction; norms of interpretation; and genres. (<strong>Discourse</strong>: connected speech or writing, such as a conversation.)</p>\n<p>Hymes then fitted the sixteen into eight groups whose first letters spell <strong>SPEAKING</strong>, “to sound fancy and effective” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>): “Hymes argues that in order to speak a language correctly, one needs not only to learn its vocabulary and grammar, but also the context in which words are used.” The model’s source, on the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1030s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:10</a>: Hymes, D. (1974), <em>Foundations in Sociolinguistics: An Ethnographic Approach</em>, pp. 53–62.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Letter</th>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Hymes’s components in it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>Setting and scene</td>\n<td>setting, scene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>P</td>\n<td>Participants</td>\n<td>speaker / sender, addressor, hearer / receiver / audience, addressee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E</td>\n<td>Ends</td>\n<td>purposes (outcomes), purposes (goals)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>Act sequence</td>\n<td>message form, message content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>K</td>\n<td>Key</td>\n<td>key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Instrumentalities</td>\n<td>channels, forms of speech</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Norms</td>\n<td>norms of interaction, norms of interpretation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>G</td>\n<td>Genre</td>\n<td>genres</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(The third column follows Hymes’s book; the lecture lists the sixteen and the eight but does not match them up.) Each component, with its slide:</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">S: setting and scene (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:40</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Setting refers to the time and place of a speech act and, in general, to the physical circumstances. Scene is the ‘psychological setting’ or ‘cultural definition’ of a setting, including characteristics such as range of formality and sense of play or seriousness.” The setting also covers where the participants are, and any physical barriers.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>So the <strong>setting</strong> is the physical where and when; the <strong>scene</strong> is the social and psychological situation: is it formal or casual, playful or serious? “Degree of formality does matter, that attaches value to what you say” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">P: participants (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1340s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:20</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Speaker and the audience that can be distinguished as addressees and other hearers. When considering the participants in a speech event, one should consider implicit and explicit rules about who is, can, and should be involved; what expectations are established for the participants; who is speaking and who is being addressed.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>An <strong>addressee</strong> is the person spoken to; other <strong>hearers</strong> may also be present. The lecturer’s example: as children, we were scolded for speaking while two elders were talking. We were present and received the message, but we were not supposed to give feedback (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>). Our socio-cultural position decides whether we may speak, what, how, and how far we may intervene (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">E: ends (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1490s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:50</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“‘Ends’ refers to purposes, goals, and outcomes.” Every act of communication has an objective, “unlike gossip”: you can gossip with anyone, anytime, about anything (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A: act sequence (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The form and order of the event. “Act sequence refers to the sequence of speech acts that make up the event. The order of speech acts greatly influences the speech event.” It also orients the participants to social cues. “Important aspects of act sequence include turn-taking and interrupting.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Who starts, who takes the lead, how and when you may interrupt or intervene, when you finish and let the other person speak: “a kind of negotiation going on continuously” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">K: key (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1665s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:45</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“‘Key’ refers to the clues that establish the ‘tone, manner, or spirit’ of the speech act.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>As a conversation goes on, the tone, the pitch and other non-verbal cues tell us its direction, its intent and its likely outcome; “we have to be very alert” to them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">I: instrumentalities (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1730s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:50</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“It refers to the forms and styles of speech. These include the method of communication (writing, speaking, signalling), the language, dialect (a mutually intelligible subset of a language) or register (a variety of a language that is used in specific settings).”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The language, the variety, the style and the choice of words are all “instrumental” in effective communication (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">N: norms (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1785s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:45</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Social rules governing the event and the participants’ actions and reaction”: When is it okay to speak? Who should listen? When is silence preferred? How loud is too loud? What speed should be used in the conversation? What topics are acceptable?</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Norms are governed by society and culture. <strong>Taboo</strong> topics (topics a society does not discuss) differ: what one culture never discusses in public may be a simple matter in another, and we feel inhibited when others talk about it freely (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">G: genre (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1925s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:05</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The kind of speech act or event”: didactic, moral instruction, gossip, jokes and conversations.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Is it gossip, jokes, a simple conversation, an intense discussion or debate, or <strong>didactic</strong> (teaching) talk? “We need to customize our conversation, our communicative style, our communicative strategy according to the genre” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A job interview through SPEAKING (my example, using Lectures 12.8 and 12.9)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>S</strong>etting and scene: an interview room or a video call, at a fixed time; formal and serious.</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>articipants: the interview board, who ask, and the candidate, who is addressed and answers.</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>nds: the board wants the right person; the candidate wants the job.</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ct sequence: greeting, ice-breaking questions, the technical round, adaptability, winding up; the candidate does not interrupt a panel member.</li>\n<li><strong>K</strong>ey: polite, positive and confident; the board’s tone tells the candidate how things are going.</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nstrumentalities: spoken English in a formal register, face to face or online.</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>orms: greet everyone; no bad-mouthing; ask your questions when invited, at the end.</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>enre: a formal interview, a structured conversation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> all eight components shape what a candidate should say and how. A good answer in the wrong key, or against the norms, still fails.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“These eight elements determine the effectiveness of our communication”: as a speaker, keep in mind “who speaks what, to whom, when, why, and how” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-a-re-sume\">Writing a résumé</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.6 (Karthika Sathyanathan) uses snippets of her own résumé. The first question students ask is: what is the difference between a résumé and a CV (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>)?</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">CV and résumé (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>CV</strong> (<em>curriculum vitae</em>) is “the long history of all that you have done”: your full work history.</li>\n<li>A <strong>résumé</strong> is the shorter version. It “generally runs for one to two page. At max, you should have two pages and not more than that.”</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Section</th>\n<th>What to write (the lecture’s advice)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Name and contact</td>\n<td>Your full name, your area of residence (<em>Chennai</em>), your phone number, then your e-mail ID (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overview</td>\n<td>A brief, precise summary of everything you want the reader to notice (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Put the institution you graduated from and the year in bold (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Publications</td>\n<td>Papers if you have them; if you are not academic, editorials for well-known newspapers or magazines: “brownie points” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Research experience</td>\n<td>Your thesis (a bachelor’s thesis of six months to a year counts) and any other research (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Awards and scholarships</td>\n<td>“Every single award and scholarship that you have got”, with the important ones highlighted (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Professional experience</td>\n<td>Jobs, and internships too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Education and skills</td>\n<td>A table: programme, institution, percentage or CGPA, year of completion. Skills: technical skills, language skills and hobbies (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Positions of responsibility</td>\n<td>Especially in college: a curriculum coordinator, a volunteer of the NSS (National Service Scheme) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(A <strong>brownie point</strong> is an extra point in your favour.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-overview\">The overview</h3>\n<p>“Your boss or the interviewer or the manager of the company will not have time to read or go through your entire CV or your resume” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). So the overview must hold every important point. The lecturer’s overview begins: “I have graduated from <strong>IIT Madras</strong> with an integrated Masters in English Studies from the Department of Humanities and Social Sciences in <strong>2015</strong>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). Its next line is marked in yellow, as a line <strong>not</strong> to write: “I am a result-oriented and performance-driven development professional with more than five years of experience” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Do not praise yourself; let the work speak</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Your qualities, it is for the manager to find out, it is for the CV to speak”: the work you have done will show your qualities. “I have seen lot of students writing, I am a very hardworking professional, which is not required” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). This is the “show, do not label” rule of the SOP in <a href=\"#/english2/w08/statement-of-purpose-stories-not-labels\">Week 8</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Be precise (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The marked line says “more than five years of experience”.</li>\n<li>But “even 20 years is more than 5 years of experience”: the phrase hides the real number.</li>\n<li>Write the exact figure: <em>5.5 years of experience</em>, or <em>8 years of experience</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> give the number, not “more than”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Passion is not expertise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Please do not confuse your passion with that of expertise.” Students write that teaching is their area of expertise; asked what they have taught, they say nothing, “but I know that I am a good teacher”. Name an area of <strong>expertise</strong> “only if you have experience in those areas” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The overview also names the people she has worked with (“bureaucrats, knowledge partners, government organisations, school principals, teachers and students”), and her research interests, a line she added when she was looking for research positions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). It blends academic and non-academic work. <strong>Aim at the position</strong>: for a managerial job, highlight your managerial work; for an academic job, your academic work (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Use different verbs</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Do not repeat <em>worked</em> in every line (“I worked for this thing, I worked in this thing, I worked for that”). Her research section uses <em>worked, conducted, rated, tested</em>: “use vivid or different verbs to describe your work” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">An overview, rewritten (my example, using the lecture’s rules)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Weak: <em>I am a very hard-working, result-oriented graduate with more than two years of experience. Teaching is my passion and my area of expertise.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cut the self-praise (<em>very hard-working, result-oriented</em>): “it is for the CV to speak”.</li>\n<li>Replace <em>more than two years</em> with the exact figure, say <em>2.5 years</em>.</li>\n<li>Passion is not expertise: name what you have actually done.</li>\n<li>Put the institution and the year of graduation in bold, and use precise verbs.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>I graduated from <strong>IIT Madras</strong> with a BSc in Data Science in <strong>2025</strong>. In 2.5 years as a teaching assistant, I designed weekly practice sets and conducted revision sessions for 120 students.</em> (The details are invented.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"position-yourself-to-win-the-star-method\">Position yourself to win: the STAR method</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.7 (Karthika Sathyanathan). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=20s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:20</a>): “Do you struggle to give concise answers to interview questions? Are you unsure how to share your accomplishments during an interview without sounding boastful? What’s the best way to let the interviewer know that you’re the right candidate for the job? The STAR interview response method can help.” “Sounding confident but not boastful is very tricky” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Behavioural interview question (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=50s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:50</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Behavioral interview questions are questions about how you have behaved in the past. Specifically, they are about how you have handled certain work situations.” The slide’s examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Tell me about an occasion when you had to complete a task under a tight deadline.</em></li>\n<li><em>Have you ever gone above and beyond the call of duty?</em></li>\n<li><em>What do you do when a team member refuses to complete his or her quota of the work?</em> (common for managerial positions, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>(<em>Above and beyond the call of duty</em>: doing more than your job requires.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The STAR method (the slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=50s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:50</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:20</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A technique to prepare “clear and concise responses using real-life examples” to behavioural and situational questions. STAR stands for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ituation: “Describe the context within which you performed a job or faced a challenge at work”, for example a group project or a conflict with a co-worker, from work, a volunteer position or any other relevant event. “Be as specific as possible.”</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>ask: “describe your responsibility in that situation”, for example finishing a project within a tight deadline, resolving a conflict, or hitting a sales target.</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ction: “describe how you completed the task or endeavored to meet the challenge. Focus on what you did, rather than what your team, boss, or coworker did.”</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>esult: “explain the outcomes or results generated by the action taken.”</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Say “I”, not “we”, in the Action</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Instead of saying, ‘We did xyz,’ say ‘I did xyz’” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:20</a>; <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). The interviewer is hiring you, not your team.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“How do you deal with a tight deadline?” (the lecture’s answer at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The answer: “While I typically like to plan out my work in stages and complete it piece by piece, I can also achieve high-quality work results under tight deadlines. Once, at a former company, an employee left days before the imminent deadline of one of his projects. I was asked to assume responsibility for it, with only a few days to learn about and complete the project. I created a task force and delegated work, and we all completed the assignment with a day to spare. In fact, I believe I thrive when working under tight deadlines.”</p>\n<p>The lecturer marks its four parts (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Situation</strong>: <em>Once, at a former company, an employee left days before the imminent deadline of one of his projects.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Task</strong>: <em>I was asked to assume responsibility for it, with only a few days to learn about and complete the project.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Action</strong>: <em>I created a task force and delegated work,</em></li>\n<li><strong>Result</strong>: <em>and we all completed the assignment with a day to spare.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the STAR story sits between an opening line (how you usually work) and a closing line (<em>I believe I thrive when working under tight deadlines</em>). (<em>Imminent</em>: about to happen; a <em>task force</em> is a small team for one job; to <em>delegate</em> is to give parts of the work to others; to <em>thrive</em> is to do very well.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"dos-and-don-ts\">Dos and don’ts</h3>\n<p>The slide “DOs” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=435s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:15</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Answer all questions using the STAR method.</li>\n<li>Maintain eye contact.</li>\n<li>Be confident, focussed and enthusiastic.</li>\n<li>Always be honest, particularly about your weakness, and add how you have been working on it.</li>\n<li>While introducing yourself, avoid the eligibility part and talk about your <strong>suitability</strong> for the job.</li>\n<li>Make the interviewers ask questions to you, and prepare for those questions in advance.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Eligibility and suitability (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Eligibility</strong> “is a bare minimum that is required for the job”: the degree, the marks, the years of experience that let you apply.</li>\n<li><strong>Suitability</strong> “focuses on how you are the ideal candidate for that particular job”: your achievements, and why you are the best of the candidates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every candidate in the room is eligible; talk about your suitability.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Leave breadcrumbs</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Give the interviewer <strong>keywords</strong> that they will pick up and ask about. Say “I believe that I am a very creative person”, and the interviewer will ask what you have done; prepare for that question in advance. “The breadcrumbs that I am going to give the interviewer ... if I prepare well for those keywords I know that I am positioning myself to win” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “DO NOTs” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=575s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:35</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Don’t</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Don’t be vague with responses.</td>\n<td>“Why should we hire you?” “Sir, because I am very hard working, I got good marks.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Don’t be too casual, overly confident or friendly.</td>\n<td>Asked how you are, do not say “I’m really good, so how about you?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Don’t forget your body language.</td>\n<td>Pay attention to it when you face the interview board (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Don’t repeat what is obvious in the CV.</td>\n<td>Not “I graduated from this college and worked in XYZ company”: they have read your CV, “that’s why they have shortlisted you” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Achievements, not job titles (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Weak: <em>Sir, I worked in this company.</em> (It is already in the CV.)</li>\n<li>Better: <em>I have led a group of 300 people.</em></li>\n<li>Better: <em>I was in a managerial position, training more than 3000 employees from different multinational companies.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> talk about “certain tangible tasks that you have done”: numbers and results, not the list of places you worked. (<em>Tangible</em>: real, something that can be seen or measured.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"interview-skills-before-during-and-after\">Interview skills: before, during and after</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.8 (Om Prakash). Years of study culminate in a job interview (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). “Communication is an art, so there is no shortcut”, and no rule 1, 2, 3 guarantees success (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). But job interviews “are all impressionistic”: they depend on the <strong>impression</strong> you make. Your rivals have more or less the same knowledge and answer in fairly similar ways, so “you need to create a lasting impression to be registered in the clutter” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). Week 7 met the interview as a <a href=\"#/english2/w07/at-an-interview\">formal conversation</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-challenges\">The challenges</h3>\n<p>The slide “Challenges” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>): anxiety; nervousness; over-cautiousness; fear of mistakes; lack of preparation; lack of control over the content; lack of appropriate linguistic skills; inappropriate communication strategies; improper body language; frequent distractions.</p>\n<p>They come as a chain (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Anxiety</strong> (“imagined fears, anxieties, apprehensions”) leads to <strong>nervousness</strong> when you enter the room.</li>\n<li>The fear of mistakes makes you <strong>over-cautious</strong>, and that leads to <strong>hypercorrection</strong>.</li>\n<li>Behind it is a <strong>lack of preparation</strong>: in your grooming, in what you know about the organisation, and in the technical knowledge the job needs. Without preparation we lack confidence and control over the content.</li>\n<li>The body shows it: eye contact, tone, pitch and rhythm (the <strong>paralinguistic</strong> features of the voice, beyond the words) become shaky.</li>\n<li>Without control over the content, you drift: “you have been asked something else, your answer is something else”.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Hypercorrection</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Making new mistakes by trying too hard to be correct (my definition; the lecture names it). For example (mine): saying <em>between you and I</em> because <em>you and me</em> “sounds wrong”; the correct form is <em>between you and me</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>This can happen at any stage, not only to freshers, and it is “quite normal and natural”. These challenges are not so big “that you cannot surmount and control them” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"three-stages\">Three stages</h3>\n<p>“There are three stages of an interview”: the <strong>pre-interview</strong> preparations, the <strong>while-interview</strong> situation, and the <strong>post-interview</strong> follow-up (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). The lecture pictures a normal, formal, face-to-face interview, not a telephone one (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"before-the-interview\">Before the interview</h3>\n<p>The slide “Pre-Interview” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=425s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:05</a>): planning and preparation; research about the organization; prepare and practise standard answers; be in control; know your audience/organization; preparation; control over the contents; organize the information; work on your appearance; arrange your papers and testimonials.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relax and be yourself.</strong> There are myths: that you must speak a lot, fast, loudly, with a smile on your face. “First and foremost, relax, you do not have to act like some other person, be your usual self.” “Acting is not advisable for an interview.” Confidence comes from belief in your <strong>efficacy</strong>: “the belief that you can do it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Research the organisation.</strong> It helps you imagine and practise predictable questions and tells you about its nature and culture. Interviewers often break the ice by asking what you know about the organisation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Prepare for predictable questions.</strong> Interviews follow a conventional structure (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>), so the questions can be guessed (the table below). “Even if we can guess certain questions, we do not do practice”: anticipate them, note them down, and practise your answers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Package yourself.</strong> “You have to package yourself as a product and market yourself.” Highlight your most interesting and salient achievements, so that the board asks more about them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Control the content.</strong> “Nobody is interested to ask you theories”: job questions are application-oriented. How is a concept applied in the real world? How does your classroom learning prepare you for the industry (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>)?</li>\n<li><strong>Grooming and appearance.</strong> Many candidates become stiff, stressed and over-formal in the room, and that causes distractions and even stammering (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). Wear formal clothes in contrasting colours, usually a light-coloured shirt with dark trousers and formal shoes; look well-groomed and cheerful (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>). “Do not be scared of being rejected. You are going there for selection” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Papers.</strong> Carry every testimonial and certificate that your application and CV mention, arranged in a folder (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The four parts of an interview (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>; Lecture 12.9 shows them in practice):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>What they ask about</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Ice-breaking</td>\n<td><em>Tell me about yourself</em>; your academic (sometimes family) background; your hobbies; your knowledge of the organisation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Technical round</td>\n<td>your knowledge, training and exposure in the field of the job</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Adaptability</td>\n<td>how flexible you are (<em>You applied for Chennai; what if we post you to Pune?</em>), whether you work well in a team and will be “an asset to the organization”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Conclusion, wrapping up</td>\n<td>your preferences: the profile, the place of posting, sometimes the salary</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"during-the-interview\">During the interview</h3>\n<p>The slide “While-Interview” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1305s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:45</a>): make a good first impression; answer the right questions; get your tone right; speaking intelligibly, fluently and confidently; daring to paraphrase; asking for clarifications; linguistic skills; interactive skills; communication strategies; ability to respond quickly; proper introduction; logical arguments.</p>\n<p>“Pre-interview is preparation before the performance, while-interview is the actual act of performance” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>). If the interviewer does not understand you, <strong>paraphrase</strong> (say it again in other words); if you do not understand a question, ask for <strong>clarification</strong>. Respond promptly, and keep your arguments logical (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"after-the-interview\">After the interview</h3>\n<p>The slide “Post-Interview” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1415s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:35</a>): follow up; thank the panel; comply with the instructions, if any, about formalities. You may be asked to complete documents, to wait, or to come for another round. “Once you get out of the room, your interview is not over yet until you are officially told to leave” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"linguistic-skills-interactive-skills-and-strategies\">Linguistic skills, interactive skills and strategies</h3>\n<p>Week 9 gave <a href=\"#/english2/w09/linguistic-skills\">similar lists</a> for public speaking. Here they are for the interview room.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Linguistic skills (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accuracy</strong> of vocabulary, grammar and pronunciation.</li>\n<li>The <strong>ability</strong> to handle a range of topics, to get into a range of contexts, and to select semantically appropriate expressions, words and structures.</li>\n<li><strong>Cohesion</strong> in ideas.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>“Grammatical errors spoil the whole mood”: they reflect your competence in the language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Pronunciation: “you do not have to sound like a Britisher or an American”. Keep your natural, neutral accent, but be <strong>intelligible</strong>, so that your pronunciation causes no misunderstanding. Do not rush: wait, form your ideas, then answer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Interviewers rarely ask a straight theory question. They give a context and ask you to respond to it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Avoid any remark that shows a <strong>stereotype</strong> or a <strong>bias</strong>. A hint of bias makes them probe further, “and you are trapped”: “make sure that you do not make any categorical remark” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>). (<em>Categorical</em>: absolute, with no exceptions.)</li>\n<li><strong>Cohesion</strong>: your ideas must be connected and related, “grounded in a context” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Interactive skills (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1665s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:45</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Fluency; speaking at natural speed; linking and pausing naturally; paraphrasing; alternate explanation; substantiating with appropriate examples; asking questions.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Speak neither too fast nor too slow: “develop the rate of your speech at a neutral speed” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Substantiate</strong> (support) your answers with examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n<li>At the end you may be invited to ask questions. Have <strong>two or three</strong> ready: about the job profile, your road map and possibilities in the organisation, or the organisation itself (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Questions not to ask</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Am I selected? Can you tell me whether I am selected? Can you tell me whether I performed better?” These are “cliche questions” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). Do not show a desperate wish to know; ask questions that show your observation and your professional skills.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Communication strategies (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1790s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:50</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Greeting; opening; holding the floor; paraphrasing; clarifying; using appropriate body language; interpreting feedback.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Greeting and opening</strong>: be polite and pleasant, greet everyone, and begin your answers on a positive note (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>No bad-mouthing</strong>: do not criticise anyone, a teacher or an old employer, and do not show strong opinions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Holding the floor</strong>: with prompt, appropriate answers you stay in control, and the interview goes in the direction you want (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Body language</strong>: no shaking, finger tapping or leg swinging while answering; a normal, relaxed posture (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Interpreting feedback</strong>: if another panel member makes a remark while you are answering, do not jump to it. Evaluate it, complete your sentence, and then address that person (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Preparing for an interview “is not a short term, shortcut way”. Start today: your language, pronunciation, grooming, a wide range of topics, and applying what you learn to real life (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"two-mock-interviews\">Two mock interviews</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In Lecture 12.9, Om Prakash and his colleagues Karthika Sathyanathan and Iyswarya act out two interviews over a video call. The lecturer warns that this is “not the real natural situation”, only a context created to show “how it goes” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). Interviews matter most at the <strong>entry level</strong>, when a fresh graduate with no work experience enters the service sector (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Each demonstration follows the four parts of Lecture 12.8: ice-breaking, the technical round, adaptability, and a winding-up round on career motivation, aspirations, expectations and the road map. The salary may be negotiated there or in a separate round (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"demonstration-1-an-english-teacher\">Demonstration 1: an English teacher</h3>\n<p>Karthika plays a teacher and teacher trainer with seven years of experience, who applies to a school (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Tell me a little about yourself: your likings, dislikings, hobbies</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>). An English teacher and trainer with seven years in ELT (English Language Teaching); she has trained more than 5000 employees of multinational companies, many of them repeat customers; she wants a more challenging teaching role; she dances, paints, and is learning Kalari, a martial art of Kerala (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Why this profession?</strong> Firstly, she comes from a humble background, sees education as her biggest strength, and wants to give back; secondly, she dreams of an educated society living in harmony; thirdly, she loves children; finally, she believes she is skilled at teaching (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Technical: how do you teach a heterogeneous, multilingual class, and with what method?</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>) She answers the second question first: no single method. Chalk and talk to teach grammar rules; TPR (total physical response: teaching through actions, mimicry and audiovisual cues, with games like <em>Simon says</em>) for drowsy after-lunch classes; a silent method, letting the students speak, for energetic classes; the Communicative Language Teaching method, focused on meaning; and a project-based approach (team work: role plays, presentations). Students from many language backgrounds are a resource, not a problem (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). (<em>Heterogeneous</em>: made of different kinds.)</li>\n<li><strong>What challenges does an English teacher face?</strong> Absenteeism (talk to the students; friendly classes and playtime) and mixed abilities (groups with both slow and fast learners, the fast ones helping the slow) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Why leave your present job? Why our school?</strong> She was a manager and discovered her love of teaching during a teacher-training programme, but her company had no teaching post. The school is among the best and gives teachers freedom in method and assessment, and friends who work there praise its culture (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Are you a team player? How long will you stay?</strong> “The best ideas are developed in partnership with others”; “as long as I can be effective to the organisation and with the students” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>How do you keep yourself updated?</strong> Courses with NPTEL and Coursera on child psychology and English language teaching (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The board closes: “the concerned person will soon get back to you with the next round of communication” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"demonstration-2-a-project-manager\">Demonstration 2: a project manager</h3>\n<p>Iyswarya plays a candidate for a project manager’s post in an educational institution (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Tell us about yourself.</strong> A Masters in Communication Systems (2014) and five years of work: she joined the IT sector through a campus drive, then moved to project management, her real interest; she discussed resources with software architects, worked on the budget team, and acted as “a bridge between the technical team and the end-users” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>How would you handle a remote team?</strong> (The demonstration was made during COVID-19.) The right technical platform; include everyone in events and meetings; clear, simple communication: “the only mantra is communicate, follow up and repeat” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>How do you prioritise your tasks?</strong> She lists her assignments from highest to lowest priority by their due dates, and sets her own deadlines one or two days early, to review before the work goes live (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>What skills does a project manager need?</strong> See the example below (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Budget management.</strong> First time and employee management; later preparing budgets for software purchases, and budget tracking with a forecast method (client expectations and past years); she kept the budget “on rails” by talking to managers when spending shot up (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Why change your job?</strong> A one-year career break for maternity leave: the right time to restart (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>What attracts you to us? How long will you stay?</strong> A top institute, a great team, her wish to work in education; “I have no plans of changing my career right now” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">From a generic answer to a specific one (the lecture’s demonstration at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Asked what skills a project manager needs, she first says: “decision making, leadership and good communication skills”.</li>\n<li>The board pushes back: “These are very generic comments, but do you have some specific idea about it?”</li>\n<li>She tells a story: a small team had to go to Pune for three months for an emergency task, which needed more money than the project’s budget (standard hotels, daily allowances, travel) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li>She checked whether the Pune branch office could lend people for a while, negotiated with the managers there, and got three local staff with related experience (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“The decision made on time was appreciated by the higher officials. So, I feel decision making is an important factor.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> a list of qualities is only a label; the story is the proof. It is the “be specific” rule of the STAR method (Lecture 12.7).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-the-board-was-looking-for\">What the board was looking for</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The four parts test four things: you <strong>as a person</strong>, your <strong>technical skills</strong> and expertise, you <strong>as an employee</strong> and what you can offer the organisation, and your <strong>adaptability and future plans</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“The hiring process is a costly affair. And any organisation that hires an employee wants the employee to stay” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Attrition</strong> (people joining an organisation and leaving it quickly) “has become a major challenge for hiring”. That is why the board asks “How long are you going to continue with us if hired?” and about your career road map (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). (The captions write <em>iteration</em>; the word spoken is <em>attrition</em>.)</li>\n<li>A fresh graduate is asked more about technical skills and command of the subject; interviews for senior posts are more interactive (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecturer’s recap:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Before</strong>: research the organisation; know the skill sets the position needs and match them with your own; if you find a gap, work on it before the interview or plan how you will explain it. Wear decent, tasteful clothes, keep good public manners, and enter with a “positive, dynamic, vibrant” feeling (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>During</strong>: a polite, audible tone; look confident; speak at a normal speed. “If you do not understand the question, do not answer with your guesswork”: ask the interviewer to repeat or paraphrase it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>After</strong>: send the documents and testimonials, and complete the formalities (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Interviews are impressionistic: in “10, 15, 20 minutes” you must package and market what your scores, background and experience already show (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>).</li>\n<li>“You do not have to be an astrologer” to guess the questions: you as a person and as a professional, your hobbies, likes and dislikes, your academic background, your experience, your projects and dissertations (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=2130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Fluent does not mean fast</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Being fluent does not equate to being a speaker with high speed”: speak normally and intelligibly, at a normal tempo (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>). And “sometimes the words may fail you, but your gesture, your posture, your appropriate body language does the trick”. Tapping the table, swinging a leg or looking here and there tell more than words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">If you do not know, say so</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Do not try to dodge a question ... If you do not know the answer, just politely say you do not know it. There is no need to attempt to fool the interviewer” with a vague answer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=2250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"tell-me-about-yourself\">“Tell me about yourself”</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 12.10 (Karthika Sathyanathan). <em>Tell me about yourself</em> is the first question of an interview. The interviewer has already read your résumé; the question is asked “because the interviewer wants to know what according to you is most important”. A good answer leaves “a strong and positive impression” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). As you walk in: greet with a smile, and sit down.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The three parts of the answer (the slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=20s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:20</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=95s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:35</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A one-sentence introduction</strong>: “this sentence should summarise who you are. Use adjectives.”</li>\n<li><strong>Three to five sentences about your achievements, plus proof of accomplishment</strong>: they tell “why you are a good fit for the job”. Be specific and give quantifiable numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Why you want this job</strong>: no negative comments on your previous company; show how your skills are aligned to their requirements.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two model answers (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An experienced teacher (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:40</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Introduction</em>: “I am an English teacher and a teacher trainer with seven years’ of experience in ELT industry.”</li>\n<li><em>Achievements with proof</em>: “I specialise in teaching Business English. I have trained more than 5000 employees of different multinational companies. Many have become repeat customers.”</li>\n<li><em>Why this job</em>: “I am looking for a job where I can put my education and skills to use, where I will be challenged to learn and grow, and contribute to the success of the company. I am very happy in my current job but I am looking for a more challenging role; one where I can use my skills in a managerial position. That is why I want this job.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A fresh graduate (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=325s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:25</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Introduction</em>: “I am a recent graduate from IIT Madras with a BSc degree in Data Science.”</li>\n<li><em>Achievements with proof</em>: “I have secured 9.5 gpa and have done xyz courses with NPTEL (related to what the company can offer you). I have interned at xyz on zyx which helped me develop my final year project. It was during this project that I discovered that I am a team player.”</li>\n<li><em>Why this job</em>: “I am looking for a job where I can put my education and skills to use, where I will be challenged to learn and grow, and contribute to the success of the company. That is why I want this job.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the same three-part frame works at every level; only the proof changes. <em>5000 employees</em> is a quantifiable number, and <em>repeat customers</em> shows that “people value the service” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A small slip on the slide (my note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s “seven years’ of experience” mixes two correct forms: the apostrophe and <em>of</em> should not both be there. Write either <em>seven years of experience</em> or <em>seven years’ experience</em> (the plural possessive of <a href=\"#/english2/w09/the-apostrophe-of-possession\">Week 9</a>), not both.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Be specific.</strong> “When I talk about my success, I should not be random and weak” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>For a fresh graduate</strong>, the achievements are from college: your GPA, courses (NPTEL, Coursera) related to what the company does, internships and projects (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Why you want this job.</strong> Do not say “my boss is bad and my co-workers are plotting against me”. Describe how your skills match their requirements (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide “Quick tips” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Always be prepared to answer “tell me about yourself”. It includes <em>Why do you want this job?</em> and <em>What are your achievements?</em>, so it needs research about the company: its work, its achievements, the job role and the experience it expects (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Don’t talk about your family or hobbies, unless they ask about them specifically (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Do not use superlatives to describe yourself: “Do not say I am the best coder in the country; that would sound ridiculous” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). (A <strong>superlative</strong> is the <em>-est</em> or <em>most</em> form: <em>best, greatest, most talented</em>.)</li>\n<li>Your interviewer might say “Walk me through your resume”. Spend only a little time on each job, and focus on your achievements (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Speak naturally; be confident and keep good eye contact; make it a conversation, not a <strong>monologue</strong> (one person speaking alone) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Hobbies only when asked</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the first mock interview of Lecture 12.9, the board asked about hobbies, so Karthika named hers (dance, painting, Kalari). In a plain <em>Tell me about yourself</em>, leave them out.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pragmatic competence</strong> (12.1): learning a language = grammatical (linguistic) competence, the <strong>I-language</strong> in the mind, + pragmatic competence, seen in performance, the <strong>E-language</strong>; they happen together. With communicative competence they build <strong>communicative confidence</strong>. Speaking is instinctive (Darwin); writing is learnt. Chomsky: language is not habit formation; imperfect, fuzzy input yields perfect learning; knowledge of language is rules we know without knowing that we know them, and adults must learn them consciously. Context is built in: <em>raajuu ke do bacce haiN</em> / <em>raajuu ke paas do gaariyaaN haiN</em>; <em>raajuu ko kalam caahiye</em>; <em>ghar aao</em> / <em>ghar aaiye</em> (informal / formal, not order / request); <em>maiN-ne kab kahaa?</em> = “I did not say so”; “I have to go shopping” = no.</li>\n<li><strong>Essays and reports</strong> (12.2): the reader has only your words. Structure, clear concepts, an outline, the reader’s questions, no over-explaining, few prepositional phrases, no fillers, practice. Know your type (argumentative, admissions, persuasive, compare and contrast, personal, expository) and your audience; edit and re-read. Report: problem → method → evidence and argument → conclusion, with a literature review and a clear research question. Research = observation → description → explanation. Thesis statement; logos, pathos, ethos; the Rule of Three; the revision checklist. Avoid too many adjectives and passives.</li>\n<li><strong>E-mails</strong> (12.3): permanent, evidence, time to rewrite, grammatical, aimed at a goal. Name in <em>From</em>; never an empty subject; <em>Dear Karen</em> or <em>Dear Dr Jones</em> (title + last name); a colon (AmE) or a comma (BrE); a succinct body; a closing (<em>Best regards</em>); a signature; empty lines between the parts. Thank explicitly. Do not reply at once or in anger; read three times; explain why you forward; write to people individually, not to a mass list.</li>\n<li><strong>Barriers</strong> (12.4): physical and mechanical; language or semantic (<em>Time is money</em> / <em>Samay balwan hai</em>; <em>Why don’t you sit?</em> = an offer); socio-psychological (stereotypes, bias, ethnocentrism); organizational (status, hierarchy, one-way flow); personal (inattention, premature evaluation, selective omission). Communication is you-oriented and two-way: turn taking and turn giving, trust, clarity, timing, feedback, empathic listening, flexibility, socio-cultural appropriateness.</li>\n<li><strong>SPEAKING</strong> (12.5): communicative competence = form + function (Hymes, 1966). Setting and scene, Participants, Ends, Act sequence, Key, Instrumentalities, Norms, Genre: who speaks what to whom, when, why and how. Every word has a spelling and pronunciation, a meaning and a context.</li>\n<li><strong>Résumé</strong> (12.6): one or two pages. Name and contact; a precise overview (institution and year in bold; no self-praise; exact numbers; expertise, not passion); publications; research experience (varied verbs); awards; professional experience; education and skills; positions of responsibility.</li>\n<li><strong>STAR</strong> (12.7): Situation, Task, Action (<em>I</em>, not <em>we</em>), Result. Show suitability, not eligibility; leave keywords; be honest about weaknesses; do not be vague or too casual, and do not repeat the CV.</li>\n<li><strong>Interviews</strong> (12.8–12.9): impressionistic. Pre-interview (research, predictable questions, packaging, grooming, papers), while-interview (intelligible, fluent, confident; paraphrase and clarify), post-interview (follow up, formalities). Four parts: ice-breaking, technical, adaptability, winding up. Ask two or three good questions, never “Am I selected?”. Fluent does not mean fast. If you do not know, say so. Employers fear attrition: “How long will you stay?”</li>\n<li><strong>Tell me about yourself</strong> (12.10): a one-sentence introduction + three to five sentences of achievements with proof + why this job. No family or hobbies unless asked, no superlatives, no complaints; a conversation, not a monologue.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Your friend says, “You said you would buy me an ice cream.” You answer, “When did I say that?” What do you mean, and which competence lets your friend understand you?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>You mean <strong>“I did not say so.”</strong> In form it is a question; in this context it is a denial. Lecture 12.1’s Hindi example is the same: <em>maiN-ne kab kahaa?</em> (‘When did I say?’), implied meaning “I did not say so”. We turn the denial into a question so as not to refute the other person’s expectation too directly.</p>\n<p>Your friend understands it through <strong>pragmatic competence</strong>: knowing what a sentence means in its social and cultural context, beyond its words. It works in English as in Hindi.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name the barrier in each case (my examples): (a) a generator roars outside the meeting room; (b) an idiom is translated word for word and confuses the listener; (c) a manager decides that a speaker from another region “will not understand” and stops listening; (d) the head office sends orders down but never asks the staff for replies; (e) a listener decides you are wrong before you finish.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Physical and mechanical</strong>: external noise.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Language or semantic</strong>: bad translation; idioms and metaphors are “frozen expressions” that rarely translate.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Socio-psychological</strong>: a regional stereotype, a bias.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Organizational</strong>: a one-way flow from the top of the hierarchy.</li>\n<li>(e) <strong>Personal</strong>: premature evaluation.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Correct this e-mail (my example): the subject line is empty; it begins “hey Dr Karen,” and then says “i want the marks report now.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Subject</strong>: never empty: <em>Reg. Marks Report</em> (capitalise every word except articles and prepositions). An empty subject makes a poor first impression and may send the mail to junk.</li>\n<li><strong>Salutation</strong>: <em>hey</em> is very informal, and a title goes with the <strong>last name</strong>: <em>Dear Dr Jones,</em> (or <em>Dear Karen,</em> if you know her well). Capitalise <em>Dear</em>. The comma is British style; a colon (<em>Dear Dr Jones:</em>) is American.</li>\n<li><strong>Body</strong>: <em>I want … now</em> sounds like an order, and <em>i</em> must be a capital <em>I</em>. Ask, give the reason, be succinct: <em>Could you send me a copy of the marks report? I would like to take it to my meeting with Rachel tomorrow.</em></li>\n<li>Leave an empty line after the salutation, and end with a closing (<em>Thanks for your help.</em> / <em>Best regards,</em>) and your name.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which SPEAKING component is at work? (a) A student keeps quiet while two professors are talking. (b) Doctors at a conference use medical terms. (c) From the chairperson’s tone, the candidate senses that the interview is going well. (d) Is this a debate, a joke or a lecture?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Participants</strong>: the rules about who may speak, and when (also the <strong>Norms</strong>: “When is silence preferred?”).</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Instrumentalities</strong>: the register, “a variety of a language that is used in specific settings”.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Key</strong>: the clues that establish the “tone, manner, or spirit” of the speech act.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Genre</strong>: the kind of speech event.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Iyswarya’s story in Lecture 12.9: an emergency task needed a small team in Pune for three months, beyond the project’s budget; she checked whether the Pune branch could spare staff, negotiated with the managers there, and got three experienced local people; her timely decision was appreciated. Split it into STAR.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Situation</strong>: an emergency task needed a small team in Pune for three months, and the extra cost (hotels, daily allowances, travel) was more than the project’s budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Task</strong>: as the manager, she had to form the team within the money available.</li>\n<li><strong>Action</strong>: <em>she</em> checked the temporary resource allocation of the Pune branch office and negotiated with the managers there (her own actions, told with <em>I</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Result</strong>: three local staff with related experience joined, and “the decision made on time was appreciated by the higher officials”.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Her first answer (“decision making, leadership and good communication skills”) was generic; the STAR story is the proof.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is wrong with this answer to “Tell me about yourself”? (my example) “I am the best coder in the country. My father is a bank manager and I love cricket. My present company is terrible, so I want to leave.” Rewrite it in the lecture’s three parts.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>The best coder in the country</em>: a <strong>superlative</strong>, which “would sound ridiculous”. Give proof instead.</li>\n<li><em>My father … cricket</em>: family and hobbies, which were not asked for.</li>\n<li><em>My present company is terrible</em>: a negative comment on the current job.</li>\n<li>There is no one-sentence introduction, no quantified achievement, and no link between your skills and the job.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A version in three parts (the details are invented): <em>I am a software developer with three years of experience in building data tools.</em> (introduction) <em>I have built two internal dashboards that about 200 colleagues use every week, and I cut one report’s running time from an hour to ten minutes.</em> (achievements with proof) <em>I am happy in my present job, but I am looking for a more challenging role where I can use my skills in data engineering. That is why I want this job.</em> (why this job)</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
